# Rank 1089 / CVE-2015-5377 (marked ** DISPUTED ** by NVD, ZDI treating it as
# the same report as CVE-2015-3253): Elasticsearch's transport protocol
# deserialized remote exceptions with no restriction on which classes could be
# instantiated, the textbook Java deserialization gadget-chain shape (CWE-502,
# tagged CWE-74 by the advisory). ThrowableObjectInputStream in
# src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/common/io/ThrowableObjectInputStream.java
# overrode readClassDescriptor() to resolve any class the serialized stream
# named, with nothing to stop that class from being one with a dangerous
# readObject()/readResolve() gadget. Pool commit bf3052d14c is the real fix; it
# adds a class-and-package allowlist (CLASS_WHITELIST, PKG_WHITELIST covering
# java.lang, java.util, com.google.common.collect, org.joda.time and
# org.elasticsearch itself) and a verify() method that throws
# NotSerializableException for anything outside it, called from both branches
# of readClassDescriptor().
#
# FIXED. The diagnosis found the shipped readObject() sink binding could never
# fire under any input: deserialization129 in vyql/bindings/java/m420f6b67.vyql
# matched callee.method == "readObject" and anchored the sink at args[0], but
# ObjectInputStream.readObject() takes no arguments -- the call always has zero
# args, so args[0] never exists and the binding was permanently inert. Measured
# directly: a handler reading a socket's InputStream into
# new ObjectInputStream(in).readObject() reported 0 findings before this
# change and 1 (VYQL-DESER-001, critical) after re-anchoring the sink at call.
# readUnshared(), the same zero-argument shape, had the identical defect and is
# fixed the same way. Both are Java's most idiomatic deserialization call, the
# one every readObject()-based RCE (Apache Commons Collections gadget chains,
# WebLogic, this Elasticsearch report) goes through, so this was not a narrow
# miss.
#
# The fix is shipped without a corresponding SafeDeserialization control for
# Java's native deserialization: the only Java core.SafeDeserialization binding
# is for SnakeYAML's Yaml.safeLoad, and no binding recognizes a resolveClass /
# readClassDescriptor allowlist override, the exact pattern Elasticsearch's own
# fix uses and the industry-standard mitigation. That means every readObject()
# call now reports regardless of whether a class filter guards it -- correctly
# matching how other SAST tools treat this call (flag unconditionally, let a
# human verify the filter), but it is a real, separately assignable gap and
# entered as such in ENGINE-GAPS.md rather than attempted here: recognizing an
# override of a specific method whose body checks a class name against an
# allowlist is a presence-analysis pattern, not a mechanical binding fix.
#
# make test is green and OWASP Java holds at +1.00 (the suite carries no
# deserialization category, so this does not exercise precision on the weakness
# class directly, but confirms no collateral regression elsewhere).
#
# verify_current_expected: fixed

test "rank1089: readObject on an unrestricted stream is a reportable sink"
  lang java
  file src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/common/io/ThrowableObjectInputStream.java
  expect VYQL-DESER-001
  code
  ```
  import java.io.IOException;
  import java.io.InputStream;
  import java.io.ObjectInputStream;
  import java.net.Socket;

  public class TransportChannel {
      public Object handleExceptionResponse(Socket socket) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
          InputStream in = socket.getInputStream();
          ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(in);
          Object error = ois.readObject();
          return error;
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1089: readUnshared on the same unrestricted stream is too"
  lang java
  file src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/common/io/ThrowableObjectInputStream.java
  expect VYQL-DESER-001
  code
  ```
  import java.io.IOException;
  import java.io.InputStream;
  import java.io.ObjectInputStream;
  import java.net.Socket;

  public class TransportChannel {
      public Object handleExceptionResponse(Socket socket) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
          InputStream in = socket.getInputStream();
          ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(in);
          Object error = ois.readUnshared();
          return error;
      }
  }
  ```
